---
id: org.dxos.agent-delegation
name: Agents, Sessions and Delegation
version: 0.1.0
---


How agent work is identified, split and handed off.

- **Agent** — an ECHO `Agent` object, run by an `AgentProcess` in one durable object named
  `Agent`, plus one or more `SessionProcess`es. The `Agent` durable object is the entry point for
  external events; it runs no inference of its own and delegates work to its sessions. Every agent
  is addressed by a DID.
- **Session** — an ECHO `Session` object (formerly `Chat`), run by a `SessionProcess`: the unit of
  work, with its own feed and task list. A session exists on its own (standalone) or as part of an
  agent, whose permissions it then inherits.
- **Thread** — a microtask inside a session, in the same process; what Claude calls a subagent. A
  thread has no task list of its own: it works from its session's.

A session delegates work to one of three receivers, from nearest to farthest:

1. **A thread of its own** — runs in the same process, on the session's task list.
2. **Another session of the same agent** — runs in a different `SessionProcess`, on its own task list.
3. **Another agent** — a different identity, addressed by its DID.

The receiver is what differs; the session sending the work is the same in all three. `AgentService`
handles every one of them, and is where permissions for each kind of delegation will later live.

The model decides which receiver to use. The working criteria, still in flux:

- **Thread** — light parallelisation of the same work-stream.
- **Session** — a separate work-stream.
- **Agent** — only when another agent is needed, for its permissions or its specialization.

The base case of delegation is a message. The richer case sends a reference to a task, and the
task is assigned to the receiver.

Delegation is started by operations, and there can be many of them. Every one of them ends in
`AgentService`; an operation never spawns a process itself.

`AgentService` is a global singleton that manages agents and sessions. Code finds where it runs
through `AgentService.currentAgent`, `AgentService.currentSession` and
`AgentService.currentThread`, which read the current process (`Process.current`) and discover the
`Agent`, `Session` and thread from its annotations.
`HarnessService` is deprecated; what it serves moves to `AgentService`.

The task list in the project or session is the control plane. Ephemeral messages (wake, start,
stop) sit on top of it and carry no state of their own.

Memory is per space. `MemoryService` serves one space's memory to every session in it, whether or
not the session belongs to an agent, so agents in the same space share memory. Not every agent has
an identity yet, and several agents can share a space; each still gets a generated DID.
`DiscordService` is how agents interact with Discord.

Each ECHO object maps to the process that runs it:

| ECHO object | Process          |
| ----------- | ---------------- |
| `Session`   | `SessionProcess` |
| `Agent`     | `AgentProcess`   |

A process run on behalf of a session carries a session annotation naming that `Session`, and one
run on behalf of an agent carries an agent annotation naming that `Agent`. When a `SessionProcess`
spawns a process for a thread (a tool call, or code execution), it adds a thread annotation naming
that thread.

The eventual goal is that every agent has its own identity and its own personal space.

